---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pageTitle, canonicalUrl, graphJsonLd, breadcrumbGraphNode, webPageGraphNode } from '../lib/seo';

const canonicalPath = '/editorial-policy/';
const title = pageTitle('Editorial Policy');
const description = 'Where exam-prep community listings come from, the role of AI, exam classification, and how corrections and removals work.';
const pageUrl = canonicalUrl(canonicalPath);
const jsonLd = graphJsonLd([
  breadcrumbGraphNode([{ name: 'Editorial Policy', path: canonicalPath }], pageUrl),
  webPageGraphNode({ name: title, description, canonicalPath }),
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-3xl">Editorial Policy</h1>

    <div class="prose-static mt-6">
      <h2>What this directory is</h2>
      <p>
        StudyGroupsHub is a directory of public exam-prep and professional-certification study
        communities on Discord, Telegram and WhatsApp. Listings are organized by exam and
        certification (SAT, IELTS, USMLE, CFA, AWS, PMP, and others), exam family, target
        market and study type. Every listing is a pointer to a community run by someone
        else — inclusion is discovery, not endorsement.
      </p>

      <h2>Where listings come from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Public discovery.</strong> Automated discovery searches publicly
          indexed sources for exam-focused community invite URLs. Only public information
          is used — we never join groups, scrape member data or read private content.</li>
        <li><strong>Submissions.</strong> Anyone can submit a public study community via
          the submit form. Submissions are reviewed manually before publication.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual curation.</strong> Maintainers may add listings directly with
          documented sources.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The role of AI and search providers</h2>
      <p>
        AI (Gemini) helps generate discovery queries, classify candidate listings, suggest
        exams and families, and summarize public evidence. Search providers surface
        candidate pages. Neither AI nor a search provider can independently establish
        truth — <strong>AI output is a hypothesis, not a fact</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Real URLs are required.</strong> A listing is only created when a real,
          publicly reachable invite URL exists and is recorded. AI never invents URLs,
          member counts, exam claims or descriptions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI never decides publication.</strong> New discoveries go to a pending
          queue for human review before anything is published.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI never scores or rates.</strong> It does not rank communities, compute
          “trust scores” or generate reviews.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Platform validation</h2>
      <p>
        Each listing records the platform the community actually lives on. Where the
        platform offers a public, official way to confirm a community exists — such as
        Discord's public invite API, which returns a guild ID — we use it. Guild or group
        IDs are recorded only from real API responses, never inferred. This lets us dedupe
        listings that share one community across different invite codes.
      </p>

      <h2>Exam classification</h2>
      <p>
        Listings are tagged with exams and certification bodies (e.g. USMLE, NCLEX, CFA,
        CompTIA Security+, CCNA, PMP) only when the community's own public materials
        support the claim — its title, description, pinned content or official links.
        Classifications follow the configured exam registry and exam-family taxonomy:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>An exam tag requires evidence; we never guess from a name alone.</li>
        <li>Families (College Admissions, English Proficiency, Medical &amp; Healthcare,
          Cloud Certifications, …) are assigned from the community's primary exam focus.</li>
        <li>Target markets (US, UK, CA, AU, NZ, IE, Global English) are recorded only with
          evidence or config-justified defaults, never assumed.</li>
        <li>Communities without a single exam focus are listed under General Study
          Communities rather than being force-tagged.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Source verification</h2>
      <p>
        Every listing stores its source URLs and verification state. Factual fields —
        member counts, dates, certification providers, study types — require real sources
        when possible. If a field cannot be confirmed, it is left unknown or empty rather
        than invented. See <a href="/how-we-verify/">How We Verify</a> for the exact
        meaning of each status.
      </p>

      <h2>Risk filtering</h2>
      <p>
        Candidates are screened for signs of harm before publication:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Exam leaks and dumps.</strong> Communities that openly trade leaked
          exam content or “verified dumps” are not listed. Promotion of stolen exam
          material is a disqualifying risk, not a feature.</li>
        <li><strong>Credential fraud.</strong> Proxy-testing services, score-fixing offers
          and certificate mills are not listed.</li>
        <li><strong>Scam indicators.</strong> Pay-to-join schemes, unverifiable “guaranteed
          pass” claims, and impersonation of exam bodies are treated as red flags.</li>
        <li>
          Listings may carry internal <code>safetyFlags</code> (e.g.
          potential-risk-language) that inform review without us declaring the community
          a scam.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Passing this filter means no obvious disqualifying risk was found — it is not a
        safety certification.
      </p>

      <h2>Manual moderation</h2>
      <p>
        Every listing is reviewed by a human before publication: the invite URL is
        checked, the platform is confirmed, the exam claims are compared against public
        evidence, and the description is written as a concise factual summary. Moderation
        decisions are made against the rules in this policy, not against any paid or
        promotional interest.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections are accepted via the report form or contact page. We review them
        against evidence and update listings when justified. We do not publish unverified
        corrections as fact.
      </p>

      <h2>Removal requests</h2>
      <p>
        Listings can be removed for: confirmed illegality, clear fraud, exam-content
        trading, spam, or at the verified request of a community owner (owner-confirmed).
        Removal requests are handled by a human. We prefer marking listings unavailable
        over silently deleting indexed URLs — dead or removed listings keep their detail
        page with an explanatory note where useful, then can be archived later.
      </p>

      <h2>Dead-link handling</h2>
      <p>
        Repeated strong failures move a link to <code>dead</code>. We don't delete a
        listing after one failed request, and bot-blocking or region blocks are never
        treated as evidence of death.
      </p>

      <h2>Content copying</h2>
      <p>
        We do not republish full third-party content. Descriptions are concise, factual
        summaries based on public evidence. Exam names and certification trademarks belong
        to their respective owners and are used only to identify the focus of a community.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising and sponsors</h2>
      <p>
        If advertising or sponsored listings are introduced later, they will be clearly
        labeled and never prioritized by payment in the organic listings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
